<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Middag</h1><p class="st-meta">1540 CE – 1732 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A multi-village alliance of indigenous peoples on Taiwan's central plain, called a kingdom by the Dutch, which kept its autonomy until the Qing crushed it in 1732.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The alliance, centred among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papora-people">Papora</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babuza-people">Babuza</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pazeh-people">Pazeh</a> and other plains peoples around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taichung">Taichung</a>, is known chiefly from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a> records of the 1640s. Its origins are obscure; it is traditionally said to have formed in the mid-16th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> described a chief they called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamachat-aslamie">King of Middag</a>, Kamachat Aslamie, who presided over some fifteen to twenty-seven villages. Villages kept their own leaders, while the paramount chief led in war and diplomacy. In 1645 the Dutch forced him to accept a treaty acknowledging their authority, though the alliance stayed largely self-governing.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Middag kept its autonomy under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tungning">Kingdom of Tungning</a> and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing</a> rule. Rising Chinese settlement and labour demands provoked the Dajia-Shalu revolt of 1731, which Qing forces suppressed in 1732, ending the alliance.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-middag.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Middag" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: PhantomFunds · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Middag_Locator.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1540 CE – 1732 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kamachat Aslamie</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous animist religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Papora, Babuza, Pazeh (Formosan languages)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/taiwan">Taiwan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Middag, Dadu Kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1636&amp;focus=kingdom-of-middag" aria-label="Kingdom of Middag on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7902 1306 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Middag lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7567 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8222 1562h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8222 1562h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Taiwan. <a href="/atlas?year=1636&amp;focus=kingdom-of-middag">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

